# Start: the systems page, focus on its first system row (where
# continue-to-systems.steps leaves it), with NO SYSTEM TICKED -- the
# precondition below. End: the CLOUD hub, after the transfer page has been
# closed.
#
# Up from the first row is the button bar on BACK. The bar has three buttons
# since SELECT ALL joined it (ES d57542662): BACK / SELECT ALL (or SELECT
# NONE) / BACK UP (or RESTORE). The first right and A press SELECT ALL; the
# second right and A press the verb. Without the first pair the verb runs
# whatever selection the picker last saved -- it saves on every close, BACK
# included -- and with nothing ticked cloud_content_backup --selected prints
# "Nothing to back up" and exits 1: the page ends FAILED with rows 3-4 blank
# and no transfer was exercised, which is how this walk shipped once (#85).
#
# The precondition, because the button is a toggle that reads as what it
# would do next: once every system is on it says SELECT NONE, and on a VM
# where an earlier walk already saved SELECT ALL this press clears the
# selection and the run fails the same way. The selection lives in
# /storage/.cache/cloud_sync/content-systems and survives an EmulationStation
# restart, so a fresh ES is not enough. Make it true rather than guess: over
# serial,
#   cloud_content_restore --set-systems ""
# empties the saved selection (a fresh image has none). The bar is rebuilt
# on the UI thread after the press, which is what the settle waits for; read
# the selected-all frame -- every switch on, the button now reading SELECT
# NONE -- before trusting the frames after it.
#
# Neither A press is re-sent. SELECT ALL is a toggle, so a second press
# clears what the first set; the verb starts a transfer, so a second press
# would start another.
#
# `settle 90` is where the old `wait 16` was. The transfer page repaints its
# elapsed time every second while it runs, so it is never still until the
# job ends and the page reads PRESS ANY BUTTON TO CLOSE -- which makes "the
# screen stopped moving" the same event as "the transfer finished", for a
# small fixture and for a real library alike. The bound is the give-up, not
# the expectation.
key up
wait-for-change
key right
wait-for-change
key x
wait-for-change 15 0
settle
shot selected-all
key right
wait-for-change
key x
wait-for-change 25 0
shot transfer-running
settle 90
shot transfer-done
key x
wait-for-change 15
settle
